  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis early this afternoon depict \nnorthwest flow aloft over the central/northern Plains in the wake of \nan upper wave traversing the Great Lakes. Meanwhile, a mid/upper \nridge builds over portions of the Northern Rockies and Central Great \nBasin. At the surface, a cold front extends from the Great Lakes \ninto the southern Plains. Despite relatively drier air behind this \nboundary, thermal profiles from short-term guidance indicate that \nradiational cooling later tonight may allow for patches of ground \nfog especially across areas north of US-50 heading into Monday \nmorning. Dense fog is not expected at this time.\n\nComfortable conditions are anticipated areawide to start the work \nweek, with seasonable high temperatures in the mid 70s forecast for \nMonday afternoon as winds come back around to the south. For \nTuesday, a midlevel impulse tracking east across the Canadian \nPrairie will shunt a weak surface trough into the Plains. This will \nserve to tighten the surface pressure gradient over the area and \npromote breezy south/southwest winds alongs highs in the low to mid \n80s. These seasonably warm temperatures will continue into Wednesday \nas the surface trough stalls over the area and washes out. This may \nhave a modest effect on temperatures, though impacts should be \nminimal as the majority of guidance to this point suggests the \nreturn of south winds by Thursday following the eastward shift of \nthe trailing surface high. Along with largely northwest flow aloft, \nthis will support continued highs in the low to middle 80s to close \nout the week. Furthermore, with this upper pattern in place, \nprecipitation chances appear minimal over the next seven days.",
